Rem ==================================================================
Rem cuttle.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: the runtime stack
Rem
Rem Shoddy's own stack exists only at weave time: Dup Swap Rot are
Rem spellings inside a Def, not values a program can hold. This machine
Rem reifies that model as a runtime value — a heterogeneous stack any
Rem mill can thread through its loop, and the pure algebra over it.
Rem The interpreter that evaluates RPN token streams against it is
Rem reckoner.shoddy; this file knows nothing about tokens.
Rem
Rem CUTTLING is the finishing operation that folds a finished piece back
Rem and forth into a layered pile — the last fold laid is the first
Rem lifted, which is a last-in-first-out store described without
Rem metaphor.
Rem
Rem NOTHING HERE ABORTS. Every popping word answers with a value: an
Rem underflow, a bad level, a kind mismatch and a matrix of the wrong
Rem shape are all returned, never raised, because a stack machine that
Rem kills the session on underflow is unusable for the interactive
Rem purpose it exists for. That is why MatAdd and MatMul are pre-flighted
Rem here rather than called and hoped for: they Error, and an Error would
Rem take the mill down with the user's stack, dictionary and registers.
Rem
Rem THE VALUE SET IS THE COUPLING. Every kind a mill can hold lives in
Rem one declaration, so this machine is the one part a new domain edits:
Rem a Cell case, a kind constant, an arm of CutShow, and an Include.
Rem That is why it names Pair, Money and Matrix, and why a caller that
Rem DESTRUCTURES one of those cells needs the owning machine included
Rem too — the cell hands back a Money, and only money.shoddy has Cents.
Rem Cases append, never insert: an old match keeps meaning what it meant.
Rem
Rem Pure throughout. Includes are bare, never As.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "seq.shoddy"
Include "str.shoddy"
Include "money.shoddy"
Include "matrix.shoddy"

Rem ---- the value set ---------------------------------------------------
Rem Closed for v1 and grown by APPENDING a case. A quoted program is a
Rem token list because there are no closures at the keyboard: a program
Rem is text that can be printed back, saved, and re-read. A pair holds
Rem two Cells, so "rate" 0.05 PAIR gives records on the stack.

Type Cell = CutNum(CNum As Number) | CutStr(CText As String) | CutBool(CFlag As Boolean) | CutList(CItems As List Of Cell) | CutProg(CTokens As List Of String) | CutPair(CPair As Pair) | CutMoney(CMoney As Money) | CutMat(CMat As Matrix)

Rem The stack itself. Top of the stack is the FRONT of the list, so a
Rem push is a Prepend and the display order (x y z t, newest first) is
Rem the storage order.

Type Stack
    CutCells As List Of Cell

Rem ---- what a word answers ---------------------------------------------
Rem Three shapes, one discipline: the failing case carries the state as
Rem it was, so a shell that matches the error still has the user's stack.

Type CutTake = CutTook(TookVal As Cell, TookRest As Stack) | CutTakeBad(TakeWhy As String, TakeStack As Stack)

Type CutOut = CutDone(DoneStack As Stack) | CutOutBad(OutWhy As String, OutStack As Stack)

Type CutCalc = CutGot(GotVal As Cell) | CutCalcBad(CalcWhy As String)

Rem ---- building and inspecting -----------------------------------------

Def CutNew() As Stack
    Stack({ })

Rem The cells in the order they would be TYPED — the last one ends up on
Rem top. CutCells reads back the other way (top first), so the round trip
Rem is CutOf(Reverse(CutCells(s))).
Def CutOf(values As List Of Cell) As Stack
    Fold(values, CutNew(), CutPush)

Def CutPush(s As Stack, c As Cell) As Stack
    Stack(Prepend(c, CutCells(s)))

Def CutDepth(s As Stack) As Number
    Length(CutCells(s))

Def CutClear(s As Stack) As Stack
    CutNew()

Rem "SWAP needs 2, the stack holds 1" — the message every underflow makes.
Def CutShort(word As String, need As Number, got As Number) As String
    word & " needs " & Str(need) & ", the stack holds " & Str(got)

Def CutPop(s As Stack) As CutTake
    If CutDepth(s) < 1 Then
        CutTakeBad(CutShort("POP", 1, CutDepth(s)), s)
    Else
        CutTook(First(CutCells(s)), Stack(Rest(CutCells(s))))

Rem ---- the pure algebra -------------------------------------------------
Rem Forth's shuffles, each answering rather than aborting when the stack
Rem is too shallow for it.

Def CutDup(s As Stack) As CutOut
    If CutDepth(s) < 1 Then
        CutOutBad(CutShort("DUP", 1, CutDepth(s)), s)
    Else
        CutDone(CutPush(s, First(CutCells(s))))

Def CutDrop(s As Stack) As CutOut
    If CutDepth(s) < 1 Then
        CutOutBad(CutShort("DROP", 1, CutDepth(s)), s)
    Else
        CutDone(Stack(Rest(CutCells(s))))

Def CutSwap(s As Stack) As CutOut
    If CutDepth(s) < 2 Then
        CutOutBad(CutShort("SWAP", 2, CutDepth(s)), s)
    Else
        Let xs = CutCells(s)
        CutDone(Stack(Prepend(Nth(xs, 2), Prepend(First(xs), DropN(xs, 2)))))

Def CutOver(s As Stack) As CutOut
    If CutDepth(s) < 2 Then
        CutOutBad(CutShort("OVER", 2, CutDepth(s)), s)
    Else
        CutDone(CutPush(s, Nth(CutCells(s), 2)))

Def CutNip(s As Stack) As CutOut
    If CutDepth(s) < 2 Then
        CutOutBad(CutShort("NIP", 2, CutDepth(s)), s)
    Else
        Let xs = CutCells(s)
        CutDone(Stack(Prepend(First(xs), DropN(xs, 2))))

Def CutTuck(s As Stack) As CutOut
    If CutDepth(s) < 2 Then
        CutOutBad(CutShort("TUCK", 2, CutDepth(s)), s)
    Else
        Let xs = CutCells(s)
        CutDone(Stack(Prepend(First(xs), Prepend(Nth(xs, 2), Prepend(First(xs), DropN(xs, 2))))))

Rem ( a b c -- b c a ): the third cell down comes to the top, which is
Rem ROLL at level 3 and is written as exactly that.
Def CutRot(s As Stack) As CutOut
    If CutDepth(s) < 3 Then
        CutOutBad(CutShort("ROT", 3, CutDepth(s)), s)
    Else
        CutRoll(s, 3)

Rem Levels are 1-based FROM THE TOP: level 1 is the top cell. A level
Rem that is not a whole number of at least 1 is refused rather than
Rem rounded, because 2.5 is a typo and answering it would hide one.
Def CutLevel(word As String, n As Number) As String
    word & " needs a level of 1 or more, got " & Str(n)

Def CutBadLevel(n As Number) As Boolean
    n < 1 Or n <> Floor(n)

Rem ROLL moves the nth cell to the top; ROLL 2 is SWAP and ROLL 3 is ROT.
Def CutRoll(s As Stack, n As Number) As CutOut
    If CutBadLevel(n) Then
        CutOutBad(CutLevel("ROLL", n), s)
    Else
        If CutDepth(s) < n Then
            CutOutBad(CutShort("ROLL", n, CutDepth(s)), s)
        Else
            Let xs = CutCells(s)
            CutDone(Stack(Prepend(Nth(xs, n), Concat(Taken(xs, n - 1), DropN(xs, n)))))

Rem PICK copies the nth cell to the top; PICK 1 is DUP and PICK 2 is OVER.
Def CutPick(s As Stack, n As Number) As CutOut
    If CutBadLevel(n) Then
        CutOutBad(CutLevel("PICK", n), s)
    Else
        If CutDepth(s) < n Then
            CutOutBad(CutShort("PICK", n, CutDepth(s)), s)
        Else
            CutDone(CutPush(s, Nth(CutCells(s), n)))

Rem ---- kinds -------------------------------------------------------------
Rem One constant per case, so a shell tags a row without writing the
Rem spelling out, and a mismatch message names both sides in the same
Rem words the shell shows. CutKindUnknown is what an unmatched case
Rem degrades to: a Cell case appended tomorrow reads as UNKNOWN in an
Rem old match rather than breaking it.

Def CutKindNumber() As String
    "NUMBER"

Def CutKindString() As String
    "STRING"

Def CutKindBoolean() As String
    "BOOLEAN"

Def CutKindList() As String
    "LIST"

Def CutKindProgram() As String
    "PROGRAM"

Def CutKindPair() As String
    "PAIR"

Def CutKindMoney() As String
    "MONEY"

Def CutKindMatrix() As String
    "MATRIX"

Def CutKindUnknown() As String
    "UNKNOWN"

Def CutKindOf(c As Cell) As String
    Select Case c
        Case CutNum(x)
            CutKindNumber()
        Case CutStr(t)
            CutKindString()
        Case CutBool(b)
            CutKindBoolean()
        Case CutList(xs)
            CutKindList()
        Case CutProg(ts)
            CutKindProgram()
        Case CutPair(p)
            CutKindPair()
        Case CutMoney(m)
            CutKindMoney()
        Case CutMat(m)
            CutKindMatrix()
        Case Else
            CutKindUnknown()

Rem ---- the display format ------------------------------------------------
Rem Mode plus digits, held by the caller and handed in: this machine has
Rem no state. STD is the default and the one a session starts in — a
Rem whole number prints as a whole number and everything else to the
Rem runtime's own ten significant digits. FIX/SCI/ENG are the three the
Rem user sets, digits meaning decimal places in all three.

Def CutModeStd() As String
    "STD"

Def CutModeFix() As String
    "FIX"

Def CutModeSci() As String
    "SCI"

Def CutModeEng() As String
    "ENG"

Type CutFmt
    FmtMode   As String
    FmtDigits As Number

Def CutFmtStd() As CutFmt
    CutFmt(CutModeStd(), 0)

Rem THE FROZEN DISPLAY WIDTH: how many columns one cell's text may take
Rem before an aggregate abbreviates. 76 is an 80-column line less the
Rem "x: " label. It is a WIDTH and not an element count on purpose —
Rem twenty-five small numbers are worth showing whole where three rows
Rem of four are not, and only a width can tell those apart.
Def CutBound() As Number
    76

Rem "... 157 more". ASCII, not the single-character ellipsis: the mill
Rem reads a UTF-8 source into one character but writes it back as one
Rem BYTE, so a saved or re-read session would lose it.
Def CutMoreMark() As String
    "... "

Def CutMoreOf(n As Number) As String
    CutMoreMark() & Str(n) & " more"

Rem ---- rendering ---------------------------------------------------------
Rem One path, so the line-oriented and row-oriented renders a shell may
Rem ask for cannot disagree: both are built from CutShow.

Rem The exponent SCI or ENG puts a number in — a multiple of step, so
Rem step 1 is scientific and step 3 engineering.
Def CutSciExp(x As Number, step As Number) As Number
    Let e0 = Floor(Log10(Abs(x)))
    e0 - Wrap(e0, step)

Rem Rounding the mantissa can carry it up to the next decade (9.999 at
Rem two places is 10.00, which is not scientific notation), and Log10 of
Rem an exact power of ten can land a hair under. One step up fixes both.
Def CutSciAt(x As Number, digits As Number, step As Number, e As Number) As String
    Let d = Max(digits, 0)
    Let m = x / 10 ^ e
    If Abs(Round(m * 10 ^ d)) >= 10 ^ (step + d) Then
        CutSciAt(x, digits, step, e + step)
    Else
        ToFixed(m, digits) & "E" & Ifte(e < 0, "-", "+") & PadZero(Abs(e), 2)

Def CutSci(x As Number, digits As Number, step As Number) As String
    If x = 0 Then
        ToFixed(0, digits) & "E+00"
    Else
        CutSciAt(x, digits, step, CutSciExp(x, step))

Def CutShowNum(x As Number, f As CutFmt) As String
    Select Case FmtMode(f)
        Case CutModeFix()
            ToFixed(x, FmtDigits(f))
        Case CutModeSci()
            CutSci(x, FmtDigits(f), 1)
        Case CutModeEng()
            CutSci(x, FmtDigits(f), 3)
        Case Else
            Str(x)

Rem How wide "{ a b c  ... n more }" comes out: 4 for the braces and
Rem their inner spaces, the elements and their separators, and the tail
Rem when there is one.
Def CutRowWidth(inner As Number, k As Number, total As Number) As Number
    4 + inner + Ifte(k >= total, 0, 2 + Len(CutMoreOf(total - k)))

Rem How many elements fit inside the bound, greedily, always at least
Rem one: a single element wider than the whole line is still shown.
Def CutFitCount(left As List Of String, total As Number, k As Number, inner As Number) As Number
    If IsEmpty(left) Then
        Max(k, 1)
    Else
        Let w = inner + Ifte(k = 0, 0, 1) + Len(First(left))
        If CutRowWidth(w, k + 1, total) > CutBound() Then
            Max(k, 1)
        Else
            CutFitCount(Rest(left), total, k + 1, w)

Rem The whole thing first, and only then a prefix of it. Fitting greedily
Rem from the start would charge every step for a marker the finished line
Rem may not carry: twenty-five primes come to 74 columns entire, but the
Rem twenty-second of them plus "... 3 more" comes to more than 76, so a
Rem walk that never tried the whole would abbreviate a list that fits.
Def CutJoinFit(texts As List Of String, total As Number) As String
    Let whole = "{ " & Join(texts, " ") & " }"
    If Length(texts) >= total And Len(whole) <= CutBound() Then
        whole
    Else
        CutJoinPart(texts, total)

Def CutJoinPart(texts As List Of String, total As Number) As String
    Let k = CutFitCount(texts, total, 0, 0)
    Let head = Join(Taken(texts, k), " ")
    If k >= total Then
        "{ " & head & " }"
    Else
        "{ " & head & "  " & CutMoreOf(total - k) & " }"

Rem Only the first CutBound() elements are ever rendered, however long
Rem the list: no element can be narrower than one column, so nothing
Rem past that could have fitted. A ten-thousand-element list costs the
Rem same to display as a short one.
Def CutShowList(xs As List Of Cell, f As CutFmt) As String
    If IsEmpty(xs) Then
        "{ }"
    Else
        CutJoinFit(Map(Taken(xs, CutBound()), Fn(c) => CutShow(c, f)), Length(xs))

Def CutDims(m As Matrix) As String
    Str(Rows(m)) & "x" & Str(Cols(m))

Rem Dimensions always, and the rows too at small sizes — a 2x2 is worth
Rem reading, a 40x40 is worth counting. Small means WHOLLY shown: a body
Rem that had to abbreviate is a body that was not worth printing, and
Rem since a matrix holds nothing but numbers the marker cannot be a cell.
Def CutShowMat(m As Matrix, f As CutFmt) As String
    Let head = CutDims(m)
    Let body = CutShowList(Map(Range(1, Rows(m)), Fn(r) => CutList(Map(ToList(MatRow(m, r)), CutNum))), f)
    If Instr(body, CutMoreMark()) = 0 And Len(head) + 1 + Len(body) <= CutBound() Then
        head & " " & body
    Else
        head

Def CutShowProg(ts As List Of String) As String
    If IsEmpty(ts) Then
        "[ ]"
    Else
        "[ " & Join(ts, " ") & " ]"

Def CutShow(c As Cell, f As CutFmt) As String
    Select Case c
        Case CutNum(x)
            CutShowNum(x, f)
        Case CutStr(t)
            Chr(34) & t & Chr(34)
        Case CutBool(b)
            Ifte(b, "True", "False")
        Case CutList(xs)
            CutShowList(xs, f)
        Case CutProg(ts)
            CutShowProg(ts)
        Case CutPair(p)
            "( " & CutShow(Fst(p), f) & " " & CutShow(Snd(p), f) & " )"
        Case CutMoney(m)
            MoneyFmt(m)
        Case CutMat(m)
            CutShowMat(m, f)
        Case Else
            "<" & CutKindUnknown() & ">"

Rem ---- equality ----------------------------------------------------------
Rem Structural, and never =: a List is a quotation and compares by
Rem identity, so { 1 2 3 } = { 1 2 3 } is False in the language. Two
Rem cells of different kinds are unequal rather than an error — asking
Rem whether a number is a string is a fair question with a plain answer.

Def CutFlatEq(xs As List Of t, ys As List Of t) As Boolean
    If IsEmpty(xs) Then
        IsEmpty(ys)
    Else
        If IsEmpty(ys) Then
            False
        Else
            First(xs) = First(ys) And CutFlatEq(Rest(xs), Rest(ys))

Def CutCellsEq(xs As List Of Cell, ys As List Of Cell) As Boolean
    If IsEmpty(xs) Then
        IsEmpty(ys)
    Else
        If IsEmpty(ys) Then
            False
        Else
            CutEqual(First(xs), First(ys)) And CutCellsEq(Rest(xs), Rest(ys))

Def CutMatEq(a As Matrix, b As Matrix) As Boolean
    Rows(a) = Rows(b) And Cols(a) = Cols(b) And CutFlatEq(ToList(Cells(a)), ToList(Cells(b)))

Def CutEqual(a As Cell, b As Cell) As Boolean
    Select Case a
        Case CutNum(x)
            Select Case b
                Case CutNum(y)
                    x = y
                Case Else
                    False
        Case CutStr(s)
            Select Case b
                Case CutStr(t)
                    s = t
                Case Else
                    False
        Case CutBool(p)
            Select Case b
                Case CutBool(q)
                    p = q
                Case Else
                    False
        Case CutList(xs)
            Select Case b
                Case CutList(ys)
                    CutCellsEq(xs, ys)
                Case Else
                    False
        Case CutProg(ss)
            Select Case b
                Case CutProg(ts)
                    CutFlatEq(ss, ts)
                Case Else
                    False
        Case CutPair(p)
            Select Case b
                Case CutPair(q)
                    CutEqual(Fst(p), Fst(q)) And CutEqual(Snd(p), Snd(q))
                Case Else
                    False
        Case CutMoney(m)
            Select Case b
                Case CutMoney(n)
                    Cents(m) = Cents(n)
                Case Else
                    False
        Case CutMat(m)
            Select Case b
                Case CutMat(n)
                    CutMatEq(m, n)
                Case Else
                    False
        Case Else
            False

Rem ---- arithmetic across the kinds ---------------------------------------
Rem What one stack value type buys: money and matrices are first-class at
Rem the keyboard rather than second-class. Numbers add, subtract and
Rem multiply; money adds and subtracts exactly; matrices add and
Rem multiply, and scale against a number; strings concatenate under +.
Rem Everything else answers with both kinds named — including matrix
Rem subtraction, which is deliberately not in the set.

Def CutMismatch(op As String, a As Cell, b As Cell) As CutCalc
    CutCalcBad(op & " is not defined for " & CutKindOf(a) & " and " & CutKindOf(b))

Rem MatAdd Errors on a mismatch, so the shapes are checked before it is
Rem called and never by it.
Def CutMatAdd(p As Matrix, q As Matrix) As CutCalc
    If Rows(p) <> Rows(q) Or Cols(p) <> Cols(q) Then
        CutCalcBad("+ needs two matrices of one shape, got " & CutDims(p) & " and " & CutDims(q))
    Else
        CutGot(CutMat(MatAdd(p, q)))

Rem The same pre-flight for MatMul, whose rule is the inner dimensions.
Def CutMatMul(p As Matrix, q As Matrix) As CutCalc
    If Cols(p) <> Rows(q) Then
        CutCalcBad("* needs the columns of the first to match the rows of the second, got " & CutDims(p) & " and " & CutDims(q))
    Else
        CutGot(CutMat(MatMul(p, q)))

Def CutAdd(a As Cell, b As Cell) As CutCalc
    Select Case a
        Case CutNum(x)
            Select Case b
                Case CutNum(y)
                    CutGot(CutNum(x + y))
                Case Else
                    CutMismatch("+", a, b)
        Case CutStr(s)
            Select Case b
                Case CutStr(t)
                    CutGot(CutStr(s & t))
                Case Else
                    CutMismatch("+", a, b)
        Case CutMoney(m)
            Select Case b
                Case CutMoney(n)
                    CutGot(CutMoney(MoneyAdd(m, n)))
                Case Else
                    CutMismatch("+", a, b)
        Case CutMat(p)
            Select Case b
                Case CutMat(q)
                    CutMatAdd(p, q)
                Case Else
                    CutMismatch("+", a, b)
        Case Else
            CutMismatch("+", a, b)

Def CutSub(a As Cell, b As Cell) As CutCalc
    Select Case a
        Case CutNum(x)
            Select Case b
                Case CutNum(y)
                    CutGot(CutNum(x - y))
                Case Else
                    CutMismatch("-", a, b)
        Case CutMoney(m)
            Select Case b
                Case CutMoney(n)
                    CutGot(CutMoney(MoneySub(m, n)))
                Case Else
                    CutMismatch("-", a, b)
        Case Else
            CutMismatch("-", a, b)

Def CutMul(a As Cell, b As Cell) As CutCalc
    Select Case a
        Case CutNum(x)
            Select Case b
                Case CutNum(y)
                    CutGot(CutNum(x * y))
                Case CutMat(q)
                    CutGot(CutMat(MatScale(q, x)))
                Case Else
                    CutMismatch("*", a, b)
        Case CutMat(p)
            Select Case b
                Case CutNum(y)
                    CutGot(CutMat(MatScale(p, y)))
                Case CutMat(q)
                    CutMatMul(p, q)
                Case Else
                    CutMismatch("*", a, b)
        Case Else
            CutMismatch("*", a, b)

Rem Ordering, for the comparison words a dictionary builds on top: one
Rem word, since > is this with the arguments the other way round and the
Rem two remaining spellings are its negations. Numbers, strings and money
Rem order; nothing else does.
Def CutLess(a As Cell, b As Cell) As CutCalc
    Select Case a
        Case CutNum(x)
            Select Case b
                Case CutNum(y)
                    CutGot(CutBool(x < y))
                Case Else
                    CutMismatch("<", a, b)
        Case CutStr(s)
            Select Case b
                Case CutStr(t)
                    CutGot(CutBool(s < t))
                Case Else
                    CutMismatch("<", a, b)
        Case CutMoney(m)
            Select Case b
                Case CutMoney(n)
                    CutGot(CutBool(MoneyLt(m, n)))
                Case Else
                    CutMismatch("<", a, b)
        Case Else
            CutMismatch("<", a, b)
